{"number": 55, "text": "                          déja     vu,    angst,       tsunami\n                          Foreign        words        in   English\n\n                          Many  English words have their origins in other languages. Some of these words are no longer\n                          thought of as ‘foreign’; for example, bungalow (Hindi origin), caravan (Persian origin), tomato\n                          (Spanish origin). Others, however, are still associated with the language they are borrowed from\n                          either because they are recent borrowings or because they keep the appearance of a foreign\n                          word. This unit looks at the pronunciation of words in this second group.\n                          Some  of these words are said with a pronunciation that makes them sound like English words;\n                          others may also be said in a way that is close to their pronunciation in the original language\n                          (marked  * below).\n\n\n                          French    words   used   in  English\n                          I’m not really au fait with the rules of cricket.   (= familiar with)\n                          The negotiations have reached an impasse, with neither side wanting to back down. /‘empa:s/\n                          or /‘empais/* (= a situation where progress is impossible)\n                          The          is put over a vowel when it is pronounced with a nasal sound.\n                          Other examples: faux pas /,fau'pa:/, joie de vivre              déja vu\n                          fait accompli               or\n                          You can find the meaning of these and other foreign words in this unit in the Key.\n                          A number  of French words in English are pronounced with a     sound:\n                          They show a  lot of avant-garde films at the cinema.           (= original and modern)\n                          Other examples: carte blanche /,ka:t'bldintf/, entre nous         or /,d:ntra'nu:/*,\n                          en route          or            nuance             or /‘nju:dins/*.\n\n                          German     words    used   in English\n                     B44\n\n                          He went  through a long period of angst during his teens. /eenkst/ (= worry and unhappiness\n                          about personal problems)\n                          Other examples: doppelganger  /‘dop*l,gzena‘/, realpolitik /rer'a:lppli,ti:k/, wanderlust\n                          /‘wondalast/ or /'vaindalust/*.\n\n                          Spanish    words    used   in English\n                     B45\n\n                          She’s an aficionado of Spanish literature.            or\n                          (= very interested and enthusiastic about the subject)\n                          Other examples: incommunicado   /,inka,mju:ni'ka:dau/, manana              E] Nino  (or El\n                          Nino)\n\n                          Italian  words    used   in  English\n                     B46.\n\n                          He complained  that he couldn’t go anywhere without being followed by the paparazzi.\n                          /,pep*r'etsi/ (= photographers who follow famous people to get pictures for newspapers)\n                          Other examples: cognoscente                 prima donna\n\n                   | 847  Japanese     words    used  in  English\n                          The tsunami killed over a million people. /tsu'na:mi/ or /su'na:mi/ (= a huge wave)\n                          Other examples: bonsai           kimono  /k1'maunau/, origami\n\n                     Bas.\n                          Chinese    words    used   in English\n                          He does an hour  of t’ai chi every morning. /,tal'tfi:/ (= a form of exercise originally from China)\n                          Other examples: feng shui          